cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its released checkpoints, please cite it."
title: "Learning When to Stay Silent: Acoustic Abstention Encoding for Automatic Speech Recognition"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-01
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/pujariaditya/acoustic-abstention"
url: "https://huggingface.co/RootAccess4Life/acoustic-abstention"
abstract: >-
  Automatic speech recognition (ASR) models can hallucinate transcripts from non-speech audio, including noise and
  music. Acoustic Abstention Encoding (AAE) is a framework that fine-tunes a pretrained ASR model's audio encoder while
  keeping its text decoder frozen. An abstention loss encourages empty non-speech transcripts, while a
  feature-preservation loss keeps speech-containing representations close to the original encoder's. AAE requires no
  speech transcripts for training and no additional inference modules. Across Whisper large-v3, Qwen3-ASR-1.7B,
  ARK-ASR-3B and Hojo-ASR-V1, AAE reduces hallucination rates to at most 0.34% on three non-speech benchmarks. With
  greedy decoding, English word error rates and Mandarin character error rates remain within 0.5 percentage points of
  the original models across six speech test sets. Ablations show that feature preservation is essential to
  suppressing non-speech hallucinations while retaining speech recognition.
keywords:
  - automatic speech recognition
  - hallucination mitigation
  - non-speech audio
  - encoder fine-tuning
  - feature preservation
